1
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,600
The Monks have been with us from the beginning.

2
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,760
They shepherded humanity through its formative years,

3
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:23,880
gently guiding and encouraging,

4
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:28,680
like a parent clapping their hands at a baby's first steps.

5
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:33,600
They have been instrumental in all the advances

6
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,600
of culture and technology.

7
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,440
They watched proudly as man invented the light bulb,

8
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:43,880
the telephone

9
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,680
and the internet.

10
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:51,320
They were even there to welcome the first men on the moon.

11
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,880
It's one small step for man,

12
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:56,840
one giant leap for mankind.

13
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:59,720
And they have defended us too.

14
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:03,320
Who can forget the time the Monks defeated the Daleks,

15
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:05,200
the Cybermen,

16
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,720
the Weeping Angels?

17
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:12,720
Two species, sharing a history as happily as they share a planet,

18
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,720
humanity and the Monks are a blissful and perfect partnership.

19
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:19,600
How lucky Earth is

20
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:25,280
to have an ally as powerful and tender as the Monks,

21
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:30,360
that asks for nothing in return for their benevolence but obedience.

22
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,680
Go! Go! Go! Get down! On your knees!

23
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:42,920
Hands on your heads!

24
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:45,000
Get down! On your knees!

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,520
Get down!

26
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,160
Search the room!

27
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,480
- Jane Bishop. - That's me.

28
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:02,320
You are charged with the manufacture and possession

29
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:05,480
of propaganda intended to undermine the True History.

30
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:09,160
This is in contravention of the Memory Crimes Act of 1975.

31
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,480
1975?

32
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:12,840
The Monks have only been here a few months!

33
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,080
Therefore I sentence you to ten years in a labour camp.

34
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:15,960
Take her away.

35
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,200
- Come on! - Be a good boy for Mummy!

36
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,240
The Monks have only been here a few months!

37
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:26,280
Fight back! It's all lies!

38
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:27,400
Get in!

39
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,400
- You know it's all lies! - In there.

40
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,920
Right, nothing to see, move along!

41
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:00,400
So, relax, do as you're told.

42
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,040
Your future is taken care of.

43
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:36,040
Resync by Yn1D www.addic7ed.com

44
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:31,960
Hello, Mum.

45
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:37,240
The Monks...

46
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,120
The Monks have only been here for six months.

47
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,880
I can't remember how I escaped, Mum.

48
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:49,280
I asked for their help and then...

49
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:51,240
And then there's nothing.

50
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,760
And then I woke up and the Monks were here,

51
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:56,560
and they'd always been here.

52
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:57,960
Come with me!

53
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,240
Everything they told us, all that we believe now,

54
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,960
is a lie.

55
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:09,760
But I don't understand it, because no-one's saying anything.

56
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:14,840
It's like... like they've been brainwashed.

57
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:17,480
With those who would do them harm

58
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:20,600
by implementing a swift and painless death.

59
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:26,040
Praise be the justice of the Monks.

60
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:28,800
I travelled with a man called the Doctor

61
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:30,200
and his friend called Nardole,

62
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,440
and I did this to save him.

63
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:34,480
I'm trying to stay strong without them

64
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:37,400
but, every day, it's harder to remember what's real any more.

65
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,320
If you suspect a colleague, a neighbour or a family member

66
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:42,560
of being a memory criminal...

67
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,600
And I know how it looks, Mum,

68
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,240
with all the stuff that he's been saying on the telly,

69
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:52,000
but it's all right, because I know he has a plan.

70
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:56,240
And one day soon, he's going to come back and save us all.

71
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:58,640
One day...

72
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:23,320
What are you doing?! It's me, Nardie!

73
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:26,240
No... Wait. Shut up. Tell me something. That first time,

74
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,040
with the Heather creature chasing us, where did we run away to?

75
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:31,800
- Australia. - What noise should spaceship doors make?

76
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:33,760
Shuck-shuck! Obviously.

77
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:38,000
Oh, God!

78
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:41,600
It happened.

79
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:42,800
I knew it.

80
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:44,640
It all really happened.

81
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:46,000
Ow!

82
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,760
- Where have you been?! - That hurt me, that really hurt me!

83
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:50,120
I needed you!

84
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:52,800
Well, I got contaminated by that bacteria in the lab, didn't I?

85
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:56,320
I was laid up for six weeks. If I was human, I would have died.

86
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,400
Right now, you'd be talking to...

87
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:00,800
Come to think of it, who were you talking to?

88
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:02,680
Um...

89
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:03,960
My...

90
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,400
Um... my mum.

91
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:08,760
I thought your mum...

92
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:10,160
Died...

93
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:12,080
You know, when you were...

94
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:13,600
Little.

95
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,080
I made up a version of her.

96
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:19,680
Yeah, I talk to her all the time.

97
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:20,960
Oh...

98
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:23,160
Well, that's not that weird.

99
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:25,080
I used to have an imaginary friend.

100
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,000
Until he left me for someone else.

101
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,720
I know, charming!

102
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:32,520
They're doing something to us. The Monks. I can't think straight.

103
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,360
It's like they're saying they've been here for ever,

104
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,560
and I know they haven't,

105
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:40,880
but part of me is starting to think that it's real.

106
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:43,920
Every day, I have to remind myself that everything that you,

107
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:46,480
me and the Doctor did actually happened,

108
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:48,680
and it wasn't just a dream.

109
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,400
Why do it?

110
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,400
That's what I don't get.

111
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,760
They invade somewhere, take control.

112
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,760
Why go to the trouble of changing the past?

113
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:59,320
However bad a situation is,

114
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,120
if people think that's how it's always been,

115
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,840
they'll put up with it. That's 90% of the job done.

116
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:07,200
Wait, you said you were ill for six weeks.

117
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:09,560
- Mmm. - What have you been doing since then?

118
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:11,040
Looking for the Doctor.

119
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:12,600
Have you found him?

120
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:15,040
Well, let's just say...

121
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:19,240
- Yes, yes, yes, I found him! - Ha!

122
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:20,920
So...

123
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:24,560
All anyone's seen of the Doctor in the last six months

124
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:28,160
- are those broadcasts he does. - He's faking it, I'm positive.

125
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:30,880
He hasn't joined the Monks. He's being held prisoner.

126
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:32,960
He's just biding his time, waiting.

127
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,760
The thing is, the Monks have got some kind of scrambler,

128
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:37,760
so no-one can trace the broadcasts.

129
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:41,880
Lucky, then, that I have got this!

130
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:45,760
It traces the source of the signal and gives a map coordinate.

131
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:47,640
Cool! Where'd you get it?

132
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:49,320
Found it in the TARDIS.

133
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:51,160
Yeah, it was in one of the drawers

134
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,640
with some old takeaway menus and 50 Danish krone.

135
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:56,000
Anyway, this is them.

136
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:57,520
So he's at sea?

137
00:08:57,560 --> 00:08:59,240
Mm-hm. Yeah.

138
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:01,800
Yeah. The Monks have got him on one of the old prison boats.

139
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:03,240
Hulks, they used call them.

140
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:05,800
- So, how do we get to him? - Every six weeks,

141
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:07,360
all their food and fuel and stuff

142
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:09,320
gets taken to them by a supply boat.

143
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:12,120
The next delivery is in two days' time,

144
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,920
off the coast of Scotland.

145
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:15,440
That's our chance!

146
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,080
Yeah, that really has to stop.

147
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:22,600
It's happening, Mum.

148
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,240
I knew it would!

149
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:27,680
So, Nardole has made contact with the captain of the supply boat

150
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:30,040
that runs to and from the prison ship

151
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:32,000
where the Doctor's being held.

152
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,640
His son is serving ten years in a labour camp

153
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:37,000
for possession of a box of comics,

154
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,680
so he's no fan of the Monks.

155
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:43,680
He'll get us on board the prison ship.

156
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:59,600
Security is really tight, obviously,

157
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:02,000
but Nardole says there's not been much in the way of resistance

158
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,400
since the Monks took over,

159
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:06,000
so they're more worried about the Doctor getting out

160
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,760
than anyone getting in.

161
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:21,360
But what we don't want is for them

162
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:23,880
to do a spot-check on our identity papers.

163
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:26,760
Papers! Spot check.

164
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:27,920
Nardole doesn't have any,

165
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:30,360
and I'm down as university kitchen staff.

166
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:50,000
It's them!

167
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:42,560
All right, what are you waiting for?

168
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,280
We need to get this stuff on board. Chop chop.

169
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:47,160
- Come on! - Come on!

170
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:04,360
After that, we're on our own.

171
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:20,600
Over the years, I have encountered innumerable civilisations,

172
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:23,280
but never one as benevolent as the Monks.

173
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:25,400
Their grace and humility...

174
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:29,600
No, their grace and philanthropy is matched only by their...

175
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:31,720
Careful.

176
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:45,480
Doctor?

177
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:50,200
Doctor, it's me.

178
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:53,960
Guards!

179
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:55,960
What are you doing here?

180
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:00,120
What does it look like? We've come to save you.

181
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:01,520
This is your doing, isn't it?

182
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:03,960
You shouldn't be here. I'll have to talk to the Monks now.

183
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:05,560
- Doctor... - Stop. Don't. Don't move.

184
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:06,480
They will kill you.

185
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:08,000
Stay where you are.

186
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:12,480
Epsilon. Fire.

187
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,240
Jupiter. Lily.

188
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,480
The Monks are on their way.

189
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,040
When they get here, let me do the talking.

190
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:23,240
Wait, what was that?

191
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:26,880
Did you actually just call them, you nutter?

192
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:28,760
You deserve an explanation.

193
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:34,520
Human society is...

194
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:37,200
stagnating.

195
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:40,080
You've stopped moving forward.

196
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:42,880
In fact, you're regressing.

197
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:45,440
- This isn't exactly much better. - It's safer.

198
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:48,200
Not so much for the people the Monks are killing.

199
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:49,520
The Romans killed people

200
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:54,280
and saved billions more from disease, war, famine

201
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:56,360
and barbarism.

202
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:58,560
No, wait.

203
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:02,040
What about free will?

204
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:06,520
You believe in free will. Your whole thing is...

205
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:09,560
You made me write a 3,000-word essay on free will.

206
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:11,040
Yes, well, I mean,

207
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:13,920
you had free will, and look at what you did with it!

208
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:16,920
Worse than that, you had history.

209
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:18,200
History was saying to you,

210
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:21,240
"Look, I've got some examples of fascism here for you to look at!

211
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:23,320
No? Fundamentalism? No?

212
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:25,440
Oh, okay, you carry on."

213
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:27,280
I had to stop you,

214
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:30,560
or at least not stand in the way of someone else who wanted to,

215
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:32,560
because the guns were getting bigger,

216
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:33,640
the stakes were getting higher,

217
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:36,360
and any minute now it was going to be goodnight, Vienna.

218
00:14:36,400 --> 00:14:38,080
By the way, you never delivered that essay, anyway.

219
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:40,640
Because the world was invaded by zombie Monks!

220
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:42,280
And whose fault was that?

221
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,360
Huh? I didn't ask for my sight back.

222
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:49,560
No, you took it upon yourself to ignore me,

223
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:51,640
to do what you thought was best.

224
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:55,440
All I can say is that we are lucky it was a benevolent race

225
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:57,480
like the Monks, not the Daleks.

226
00:14:57,480 --> 00:14:59,680
Yes, I know the Monks are ruthless,

227
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:01,840
I get that. Yes, they play with history

228
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:04,360
and I'm not exactly thrilled about that,

229
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:07,600
but they bring peace and order.

230
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,480
Ok.

231
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:13,480
Yeah, yeah, I get it.

232
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:17,200
It's like that time we discovered that huge fish creature

233
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:19,360
in the Seine in Paris.

234
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:26,800
That was a coded message.

235
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:29,960
The big fish creature was under the Thames.

236
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:34,400
If I'd played along, she'd have known that I was tricking you.

237
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:36,600
Guns down.

238
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:41,480
I'm sorry, Bill.

239
00:15:41,480 --> 00:15:43,120
I just... I just...

240
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:45,400
I really wanted to make you see.

241
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:53,040
This...

242
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:55,840
This is real.

243
00:15:57,560 --> 00:15:59,240
You...

244
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:00,520
You're actually...

245
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:02,480
You're actually doing this.

246
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,200
Do you have any idea

247
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:11,560
how hard the past few months have been?

248
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,000
How hard it's been to hold onto to the truth?

249
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:21,240
It would have been so easy to just give in

250
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:23,040
and believe their lies.

251
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:25,480
But I didn't.

252
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:31,320
I fought against it.

253
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:32,640
For you!

254
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:35,080
For when you came back.

255
00:16:35,520 --> 00:16:37,840
And now you're saying you've joined them?

256
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:41,560
You're helping them?

257
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:54,720
Bill, put the gun down.

258
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:56,440
I'm serious, Doctor.

259
00:16:57,800 --> 00:16:59,960
We'll think of something else.

260
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:04,120
But you better tell me now.

261
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:07,920
Because if you help the Monks,

262
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:10,320
then nothing will ever stop them.

263
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:12,440
They'll be here for ever.

264
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:16,360
It's not a trick, it's not a plan. I have joined the Monks.

265
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:18,880
Whatever it takes,

266
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:21,440
I'm going to save you from yourselves.

267
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:04,120
Good girl!

268
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:11,480
Regeneration a little bit too much?

269
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:12,840
No, I thought it was a nice touch.

270
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:16,160
"Too much" was Richard asking for our identity papers.

271
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:18,160
I couldn't resist it. Your face.

272
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:19,840
Er, hello?

273
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,760
Could somebody tell me what the hell is going on?

274
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:24,160
You did it.

275
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:26,240
You did it!

276
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:29,560
The Monks are using some kind of...

277
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,440
you know, something,

278
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,240
to control the population.

279
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:35,760
They must be, otherwise they wouldn't have been able to

280
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:37,760
hold on to power for this long.

281
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:39,920
Now, they don't trust me yet, not completely,

282
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:42,560
and I had to just check that you weren't under the influence

283
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:44,560
- and testing me. - So you...

284
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:46,200
You... you haven't turned?

285
00:18:46,200 --> 00:18:48,160
You're not working for them?

286
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:52,720
No, of course not!

287
00:18:52,720 --> 00:18:55,240
I've spent the last six months planning,

288
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,280
and also recruiting all these chaps.

289
00:18:57,320 --> 00:19:00,920
Deprogramming them one by one,

290
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:03,080
talking some sense into them.

291
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:05,680
And there's loads of them, I could do with a Strepsil!

292
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:07,400
But I shot you.

293
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:09,320
Yes, well, that was the plan, you see?

294
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:12,280
Everyone exchanged their ammo for blanks.

295
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:14,480
- Ahem. - Did you forget, Dave?

296
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:16,160
You forgot?

297
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:19,400
Well, that would have really blown the plan, wouldn't it?

298
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:20,960
But you called the Monks.

299
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:22,440
I called the kitchen.

300
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:24,680
Oh, could you pop down and explain it to them?

301
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:27,200
They're going to be really confused.

302
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:31,000
